      <p>
        Walk the loop with a real one. The fix for the second Orai vendor
        switch took hours, because we only had to handle one missing field.
        Everything <em>around</em> the fix took weeks. We answered the angry
        reviews, cleared the support queue, and won back users who&rsquo;d
        been given nonsense feedback. Each of those jobs interrupted the team,
        split its attention, and raised the load that causes the next miss.
        When I reviewed one batch of these files, <b>three out of four took a
        day or two at most to fix</b> once someone actually looked. A blind
        spot never costs you the fix. It costs you the months when nobody was
        positioned to look.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is why startups slow down while everyone works harder. Big
        dramatic failures don&rsquo;t kill velocity. Rework loops do, and each
        one looked like a one-off. Each was &ldquo;impossible to
        predict,&rdquo; and each sat in plain sight of somebody who
        wasn&rsquo;t positioned to look. The classic industry estimate says
        avoidable rework eats <b>40&ndash;50% of total project
        effort</b>.<sup><a href="#src-6">6</a></sup> On a ten-person team,
        that&rsquo;s four or five salaries spent redoing work nobody caught
        the first time. An engineer I trust treats it as literal finance.
        Attention debt has a principal and an interest rate, and every rework
        loop is <em>interest due</em>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Early-stage teams are the blindest by structure, because the things
        that make startups fast are the things that push attentional load
        highest. <b>Solo ownership</b> puts one pair of eyes on each system,
        so nothing gets looked at twice. <b>Context switching</b> never stops.
        <b>Demo pressure</b> aims everyone&rsquo;s attention at the same
        pixels every Friday. And nobody remembers past gorillas, because the
        company is too young to have <b>institutional memory</b>.
      </p>
    </PostSection>
    <PostSection id="triad" no="05" label="The fix" title="One developer, two watchers">
      <p class="lede">
        A decade of case files, at Orai, again at CopyCat and again now,
        pushed me to one conclusion. <b>A developer is high-performance only
        inside a triangle.</b> A technical peer keeps the code honest, and a
        non-technical standard holder keeps the work aimed.
      </p>

      <figure class="diagram post-panel" role="img" aria-label="Triangle diagram: a developer supported by a technical peer watching channel 1 (technical misses) and a standard holder watching channel 2 (alignment misses).">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 680 330" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" font-family="var(--font-ui)">
          <!-- triangle edges -->
          <line x1="340" y1="66" x2="150" y2="240" stroke="var(--color-line)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <line x1="340" y1="66" x2="530" y2="240" stroke="var(--color-line)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <line x1="150" y1="240" x2="530" y2="240" stroke="var(--color-line)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5 5"/>
          <!-- developer -->
          <rect x="255" y="18" width="170" height="56" rx="10" fill="var(--color-surface)" stroke="var(--color-text-soft)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <text x="340" y="42" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-text)" font-size="13.5" font-weight="500">the developer</text>
          <text x="340" y="61" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-muted)" font-size="11">budget spent on building</text>
          <!-- peer -->
          <rect x="40" y="240" width="230" height="72" rx="10" fill="var(--color-surface)" stroke="var(--color-accent)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <text x="155" y="264" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-accent)" font-size="11" letter-spacing="1.5">TECHNICAL PEER</text>
          <text x="155" y="284" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-text)" font-size="12">&ldquo;what does this change</text>
          <text x="155" y="300" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-text)" font-size="12">that you didn&rsquo;t intend?&rdquo;</text>
          <!-- standard holder -->
          <rect x="410" y="240" width="240" height="72" rx="10" fill="var(--color-surface)" stroke="var(--color-teal)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <text x="530" y="264" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-teal)" font-size="11" letter-spacing="1.5">STANDARD HOLDER</text>
          <text x="530" y="284" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-text)" font-size="12">&ldquo;is this the thing</text>
          <text x="530" y="300" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-text)" font-size="12">we actually needed?&rdquo;</text>
          <!-- channel tags beside the edges -->
          <rect x="52" y="152" width="158" height="40" rx="10" fill="var(--color-accent)" opacity="0.12"/>
          <text x="131" y="169" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-accent)" font-size="10.5">covers channel 1</text>
          <text x="131" y="184" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-muted)" font-size="10.5">review &middot; pairing</text>
          <rect x="470" y="152" width="170" height="40" rx="10" fill="var(--color-teal)" opacity="0.14"/>
          <text x="555" y="169" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-teal)" font-size="10.5">covers channel 2</text>
          <text x="555" y="184" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-muted)" font-size="10.5">demos &middot; definition of done</text>
        </svg>
      </figure>

      <p>
        <b>The technical peer</b> doesn&rsquo;t ask &ldquo;is this good
        code?&rdquo; They ask <em>what does this change do that you
        didn&rsquo;t intend?</em> They bring a fresh attention budget to your
        diff. They see the deleted guard clause while you&rsquo;re checking
        the rename, and the ten-minute assumption while you&rsquo;re admiring
        the retry logic. Decades of inspection research agree with the gorilla
        lab. A second, independent reader finds defects the author
        can&rsquo;t, because the author&rsquo;s intent gets in the way.<sup><a
        href="#src-5">5</a></sup> Attention is a budget for the reviewer too,
        so the best industrial data comes with a speed limit. Past roughly 400
        lines an hour, reviewers find far fewer defects.<sup><a
        href="#src-5">5</a></sup> A skimmed review is a gorilla video on
        fast-forward. At one company I&rsquo;ve watched, an engineer publicly
        called the team&rsquo;s best reviewer &ldquo;the single largest
        multiplier to my personal productivity.&rdquo; Reviews don&rsquo;t
        demo well, but they compound.
      </p>
      <p class="aside">
        Worried the second set of eyes slows you down? The radiology study has
        a detail for you. The radiologists who <em>did</em> spot the gorilla
        performed no worse at finding nodules.<sup><a
        href="#src-2">2</a></sup> Catching the anomaly and doing the primary
        job only conflict when one person under load tries to do both alone. A
        reviewer of this draft asked a fair question: &ldquo;yeah, but how
        much slower?&rdquo; The study didn&rsquo;t clock them, but the
        review-speed data above says slower <em>is</em> the point.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>The non-technical standard holder</b> might be a PM, an ops lead, a
        domain expert or a founder. They never read the diff, and that&rsquo;s
        exactly why they help. <em>They aren&rsquo;t hunting nodules, so they
        see gorillas right away.</em> They look at the invoice, not the code
        that generates it. That&rsquo;s why, in the case files above, the
        person who finally saw the miss so often sat outside engineering. They
        ask <em>is this the thing we needed?</em>, and they have to ask it
        early and often, not at the end. They hold the standard, which says
        what done means, what correct means, and whose definition of correct
        wins when two departments disagree. The fix that unbalanced the books
        happened because nobody held that standard.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>This is also the engineering case for diverse teams.</b> I mean the
        velocity case, not the values-poster one. Homogeneous teams
        don&rsquo;t fail randomly. They fail <em>identically</em>. Twenty
        radiologists didn&rsquo;t miss twenty different things. They all
        missed the <em>same</em> gorilla, because they&rsquo;d all been
        trained onto the same template. AI produced the most public example of
        this in years. Commercial facial-analysis systems misclassified
        darker-skinned women at error rates up to <b>34.7%</b>, while their
        error rate for lighter-skinned men stayed under 1%. The benchmarks
        behind them were roughly four-fifths lighter-skinned faces, the teams
        that built and tested them never noticed, and the person who caught it
        was an MIT grad student the systems kept failing on.<sup><a
        href="#src-8">8</a></sup> That&rsquo;s a case file from this essay at
        industry scale. The gorilla was invisible to everyone whose face
        matched the demo path. Fresh eyes aren&rsquo;t a side benefit of
        diversity. They&rsquo;re how it works. People from different
        backgrounds run different attention templates, which is why Fei-Fei Li
        co-founded AI4ALL around the question &ldquo;AI will change the world.
        Who will change AI?&rdquo; A team that all looks, trains and thinks
        alike is one attention system with extra keyboards.
      </p>
      <p>
        None of this means &ldquo;add process.&rdquo; It&rsquo;s two
        relationships. It also fits one of the oldest findings in software
        engineering. A problem found after delivery often costs 100 times more
        to fix than one caught during requirements and design.<sup><a
        href="#src-6">6</a></sup> The attention research adds that nobody,
        however senior, can inspect their own blind spot. Talent doesn&rsquo;t
        fix attention. <em>Coverage</em> does.
      </p>

      <h3>What it looks like when it works</h3>
      <p>
        None of this requires a reorg. The triangle is a rotation, not a hire.
        Across three companies, it got recast per problem instead of per
        title. At Orai I took the standard-holder corner as COO on some
        problems and the technical corner on others, and our CEO or CTO took
        whichever corner I wasn&rsquo;t in. Every cast had one builder, one
        fresh technical eye, and one person who wouldn&rsquo;t let &ldquo;it
        runs&rdquo; pass for &ldquo;it&rsquo;s right.&rdquo; Here are nine
        that actually ran.
      </p>

