              <text x="10" y="142" text-anchor="start" fill="var(--color-text-soft)">CTO</text>
              <text x="210" y="142" text-anchor="end" fill="var(--color-text-soft)">marketer</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <div class="mini-triad"><span class="mt-co">CopyCat</span>
            <svg viewBox="0 0 220 158" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" font-family="var(--font-ui)" font-size="11.5">
              <line x1="110" y1="36" x2="34" y2="122" stroke="var(--color-line)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <line x1="110" y1="36" x2="186" y2="122" stroke="var(--color-line)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <line x1="34" y1="122" x2="186" y2="122" stroke="var(--color-line)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/>
              <circle cx="110" cy="36" r="5" fill="var(--color-text-soft)"/>
              <circle cx="34" cy="122" r="5" fill="var(--color-accent)"/>
              <circle cx="186" cy="122" r="5" fill="var(--color-teal)"/>
              <text x="110" y="22" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--color-text)">me &mdash; CAIO</text>
              <text x="10" y="142" text-anchor="start" fill="var(--color-text-soft)">developer</text>
              <text x="210" y="142" text-anchor="end" fill="var(--color-text-soft)">PM</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="triad-legend">
          <span class="dot dot-b"></span>builder&ensp;
          <span class="dot dot-p"></span>fresh technical eye&ensp;
          <span class="dot dot-s"></span>standard holder
        </p>
      </figure>
      <p>
        When the triangle is running, the fix is rarely what you&rsquo;d
        expect, and the timeline is rarely the one you&rsquo;ve learned to
        budget for. Orai spent a year and a half working hard and going
        sideways. We shipped constantly, and the metrics stayed flat. Then
        came Techstars, and with it the loop that mattered. A designer, a
        developer and a founder worked week after week on the first lesson a
        new user ever saw. In about three months, completion of that lesson
        went from <b>roughly 10% to 95%</b>, as best I can reconstruct it. Our
        old archives back the starting number, not the peak. The team and the
        effort stayed the same, and none of the changes were speech
        technology. We added a banner telling people to hit record. We cut
        text. We swapped a static walkthrough for an animated one. We added a
        gamified loop, and it was the developer&rsquo;s idea, not the
        designer&rsquo;s, which is the whole point. We simplified the results
        screen and put &ldquo;redo this lesson&rdquo; and &ldquo;next
        lesson&rdquo; where a thumb actually lands. Every change was small and
        unglamorous, and none of them belonged to one corner of the triangle.
      </p>
      <p>
        CopyCat&rsquo;s version has a sharper edge and the same timeline.
        We&rsquo;d gone a year without that loop, and the production model sat
        stale for months while everyone worked hard on everything else. Then a
        manual audit in late 2022 put our component-detection accuracy between
        <b>29% and 75%</b>, depending on element type. On a good day, that was
        a coin flip. Our performance marketer ran the structured tests,
        because fresh eyes don&rsquo;t care about job titles, and ML engineers
        fixed what he found. Then progress stalled at 75%, which is where
        progress usually stalls, with the entirely reasonable question
        <em>&ldquo;it&rsquo;s good enough, no?&rdquo;</em> It wasn&rsquo;t.
        Below roughly 95%, a missed component didn&rsquo;t just lower quality.
        It turned into broken generated code in everything built on top of it.
        I was holding the standard, so I showed exactly that, and I kept the
        bar where it was. That isn&rsquo;t code review. It&rsquo;s refusing to
        let &ldquo;good enough&rdquo; quietly redefine <em>done</em> halfway
        up. The developer&rsquo;s skill is what actually closed the gap. They
        took accuracy from <b>75% to roughly 95%</b>, and the verdict flipped
        with it. Users called it &ldquo;a different league,&rdquo; said false
        positives were &ldquo;near absent,&rdquo; and told us it was
        &ldquo;exactly what we need.&rdquo; From the first audit to that last
        verdict took about three months with the loop, after a year without
        one. The skill closed the gap. The standard kept the gap visible long
        enough to close.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here&rsquo;s a smaller, more recent one, from a company I&rsquo;ll
        keep anonymous. It&rsquo;s an engineer&rsquo;s first week. On day one,
        they joined. On day two, they sat with the ops team and listened to
        them complain about one screen. On days three and four, they fixed it.
        On day five, the ops lead discovered the fix live, mid-demo. The same
        team later called a bigger rebuild of that console a total
        game-changer, and their daily use of it quietly steered what got built
        next. When the same habit pointed outward, a customer once wrote in
        unprompted: &ldquo;oh wow &mdash; that is easy.&rdquo;
      </p>
      <p>
        This is how a team works through uncertainty before it has process.
        The standard holder holds the moving target, the peer holds
        correctness, and the builder builds. Then the numbers move. Notice
        what didn&rsquo;t change. In both companies, the same people worked
        the same hours through the stagnant stretch and through the sprint.
        Effort was never the variable. Structure was.
      </p>
    </PostSection>
    <PostSection id="automation" no="06" label="The objection" title="“Shouldn’t a machine be watching this?”">
      <p class="lede">
        The sharpest response to a draft of this essay came from an engineer
        I trust, and it deserves to be answered at full strength.
      </p>
      <p>
        He argued that this is a very human-centric post and that the problem
        is bigger than any team&rsquo;s attention. Some systems are already
        too large for any one owner to keep in their head, which is why mature
        industries stopped relying on awareness. Your car diagnoses itself
        over a bus protocol, and no mechanic finds the fault by &ldquo;paying
        more attention.&rdquo; Manufacturing didn&rsquo;t fix human error by
        hiring more careful humans. It built jidoka, andon cords and
        pointing-and-calling. Humans are inherently non-deterministic.
        <em>Today I pay attention, tomorrow I don&rsquo;t.</em> In his words,
        the structure I&rsquo;m proposing is &ldquo;a sliver along a mountain
        of automations that is required.&rdquo;
      </p>
      <p>
        And he&rsquo;s right, embarrassingly so, case by case. Walk the files
        back through his eyes. An acceptance test catches the wiped pricing
        rules. Anomaly alerts catch the pipeline that lost weeks of records.
        One end-to-end test catches the logged-out crash. A linter catches the
        trailing space. Contract tests on the response shape catch the vendor
        switch. <b>Every gorilla in this essay has a cage a machine could
        build.</b>
      </p>
      <p>
        So why isn&rsquo;t this essay called &ldquo;write more tests&rdquo;?
        Because I left something out of the first draft, which is ironic in an
        essay about the things we leave out. <b>Startups are pre-process.</b>
        On day 30 you own none of that mountain, and people still expect you
        to deliver as if you had six-sigma discipline. Worse, before
        product&ndash;market fit, the definition of <em>correct</em> is still
        moving. Half the checks you&rsquo;d lovingly write today would guard a
        building you&rsquo;re about to tear down. Channel 2 doesn&rsquo;t just
        slip past tests. It makes them obsolete. You can&rsquo;t test your way
        out of not yet knowing what true means.
      </p>
      <p>
        The triangle isn&rsquo;t a consolation prize while you wait for real
        infrastructure, either. The fastest delivery I&rsquo;ve watched
        recently ran as a triangle precisely <em>because</em> there was no
        process to lean on. Everyone had kicked a cross-cutting migration down
        the road for months. Its tentacles reached all over the most complex
        code, and there were many ways to get it wrong. It shipped in <b>nine
        working days instead of the month it &ldquo;should&rdquo; have
        taken</b>. Two developers built it, a reviewer who knew where the
        tentacles were checked it, and the business side held a hard deadline.
        Coverage made it fast.
      </p>
      <p>
        The deeper answer is that <b>automation is crystallized attention</b>.
        A person had to imagine every one of those cages first. A test is a
        falsifiable hypothesis about your own code. My reviewer asks a
        question most of us are too proud to ask, which is <em>&ldquo;what
        should be true for this to fail?&rdquo;</em> Someone has to see the
        failure mode before anyone can encode it. Tests guard the paths
        you&rsquo;ve already imagined, and fresh attention finds the ones you
        haven&rsquo;t. That isn&rsquo;t humans versus machines. It&rsquo;s an
        assembly line with two stations, and the human one is upstream.
      </p>
      <p>
        Beyond the bugs it catches, the triangle produces <em>hypotheses worth
        encoding</em>. Run the root-cause ritual honestly, and ask what you
        were staring at and who was positioned to see it. Every answer that
        ends in &ldquo;a machine could watch this&rdquo; becomes this
        sprint&rsquo;s test or alert. That&rsquo;s the whole path from
        pre-process to process. <b>The triangle isn&rsquo;t the alternative to
        the automation mountain. It&rsquo;s the quarry.</b> Relentless
        alignment produces process as a byproduct. Pointing-and-calling was a
        human ritual long before it was a system, and the ritual is how anyone
        learned what to systematize.
      </p>
      <p>
        My reviewer named the thinker behind all of this, and engineering
        education mostly skips him. It&rsquo;s Karl Popper. Doctors meet
        philosophy early, because it directly affects their patients.
        Engineers are presumed to &ldquo;work with things,&rdquo; so we pick
        up falsifiability on the job or never. But software is a knowledge
        discipline wearing a hard hat. The reason we don&rsquo;t write
        falsification tests is the same reason we miss the gorilla.
        We&rsquo;re blind to our blindness, and admitting that is where every
        real process, human or machine, begins.
      </p>
    </PostSection>
    <PostSection id="practice" no="07" label="The practice" title="Relentless alignment">
      <p>
        The triangle only works if its three people talk often. Alignment
        isn&rsquo;t a kickoff meeting, and it decays under load like
        everything else. The teams I&rsquo;ve watched stay fast treat it as a
        rhythm of small, boring, cheap corrections. Steal this:
      </p>

      <Checklist
        storageKey="ib-post-rituals.v1"
        markdownTitle="Relentless alignment — the rhythm"
        items={rituals}
      />

      <p>
        Each of these costs minutes, and that&rsquo;s the point. A correction
        in a demo costs a conversation. The same miss, caught by a customer,
        costs an incident, and as the loop above shows, it costs the next
        incident too. Relentless doesn&rsquo;t mean intense. It means
        <em>frequent</em>. A helmsman makes tiny corrections constantly,
        because without them the boat doesn&rsquo;t hold a straight line. It
        arrives at the wrong port.
      </p>
    </PostSection>
    <PostSection id="close" no="08" label="The point" title="You can’t out-focus blindness">
      <p>
        Focus is not the cure for inattentional blindness. Focus is the
        <em>cause</em>. Effort spends the same budget, and so does caring.
        Seniority just spends it more efficiently on the primary task. The
        only fix that survives a real sprint is structural. <b>You need more
        than one attention system, each pointed at a different question, and
        you need to re-sync them relentlessly.</b>
      </p>
      <p>
        That means one developer, a technical peer who notices when the code
        drifts from intent, and a non-technical standard holder who notices
        when intent drifts from need. It isn&rsquo;t overhead. Measured
        against the rework loop, it&rsquo;s the fastest setup I&rsquo;ve seen
        in ten years of startups.
      </p>
      <p>
        The gorilla is already in your codebase. It&rsquo;s in mine. The only
        question is whether anyone on your team is positioned to see it, or
        whether your customers have become your standard holders.
      </p>
      <p class="cta">
        If this matches your experience, or you have a counterexample, I want
        to hear it.
      </p>
    </PostSection>
